          viewing_task:
            '<ol><li><strong>[02:00] Recall:</strong> Name the department store lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina where four Black college students staged the first famous sit-in on 1 February 1960.</li><li><strong>[04:30] Explain:</strong> Describe the rigorous non-violent direct-action training developed by James Lawson in Nashville: why were students trained to endure physical assault and insults without retaliating?</li><li><strong>[07:15] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate how the creation of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC) in April 1960 marked a decisive generational shift away from the traditional NAACP and SCLC leadership styles.</li></ol>',
          model_answer:
            '<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> F.W. Woolworth store in Greensboro.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Rev. James Lawson organized workshops where students role-played being spat upon, shoved, having hot coffee poured on them, and hearing racial slurs. This psychological hardening ensured that during actual sit-ins, students remained totally peaceful, dignified, and silent. Televised contrasts between well-dressed, polite Black students studying at counters and vicious white mobs beating them stripped segregation of any moral defense.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Under the guidance of Ella Baker, SNCC rejected the hierarchical, preacher-dominated top-down model of Martin Luther King Jr.\'s SCLC and the slow legalism of Roy Wilkins\' NAACP. SNCC championed grassroots "group-centered leadership", empowering youth and local rural Southern communities to organize their own voter registration drives, despite extreme danger.</li></ol>',
