          viewing_task:
            '<ol><li><strong>[01:10] Recall:</strong> What was the bridge named in Selma, Alabama, where 600 civil rights marchers led by John Lewis and Hosea Williams were ambushed by mounted state troopers on 7 March 1965?</li><li><strong>[02:30] Explain:</strong> Explain the key enforcement mechanisms introduced by the Voting Rights Act of 1965 that permanently dismantled Southern voter suppression.</li><li><strong>[03:30] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate why Selma was the political catalyst for the Voting Rights Act: why was legislative action on voting rights impossible to delay after ABC interrupted its movie broadcast to show the footage?</li></ol>',
          model_answer:
            '<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The Edmund Pettus Bridge (named after a Confederate general and Grand Dragon of the Alabama Ku Klux Klan).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The 1965 Act outlawed all discriminatory literacy tests and voter comprehension devices across the United States. Crucially, Section 5 empowered federal examiners to travel into Southern counties where registration was below 50% to directly register Black voters under federal protection, while requiring Southern states with histories of discrimination to obtain federal "preclearance" before changing any voting laws.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> ABC interrupted its prime-time national broadcast of the film <em>Judgment at Nuremberg</em> (about Nazi war crimes) to air raw footage of Alabama troopers beating peaceful American citizens with nightsticks and tear gas. The moral shock of seeing Nazi atrocities juxtaposed with American state violence sparked nationwide protests, giving President Lyndon B. Johnson the political capital to address a joint session of Congress with the words "And we shall overcome", passing the act within five months.</li></ol>',
