          viewing_task:
            '<ol><li><strong>[01:00] Recall:</strong> Name two common booby traps manufactured by the Vietcong from discarded US artillery shells, scrap metal, and bamboo stakes.</li><li><strong>[02:15] Explain:</strong> Describe the engineering features of the 150-mile Cu Chi tunnel complex (hospitals, kitchens, sleeping quarters) and why US chemical defoliants (Agent Orange) and napalm failed to destroy them.</li><li><strong>[03:40] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate why General William Westmoreland\'s strategy of "attrition" (measuring military success by "body count") proved a fatal strategic error in Vietnam.</li></ol>',
          model_answer:
            '<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Punji stake pits (sharpened bamboo stakes smeared with human excrement to cause gangrene) and "bouncing betty" landmines or toe-popper explosive cartridges.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The Cu Chi tunnels featured multi-level labyrinthine engineering with airtight trapdoors, U-shaped water locks to block tear gas, and disguised smoke vents that filtered cooking smoke miles away in the jungle. They were dug up to 30 feet underground through dense clay, rendering them virtually immune to conventional bombing. While Agent Orange stripped foliage and napalm incinerated the surface, Vietcong fighters survived safely underground, emerging behind US patrols to stage devastating ambushes.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Westmoreland assumed that superior American firepower could kill Vietcong and North Vietnamese soldiers faster than North Vietnam could replace them. However, Ho Chi Minh correctly understood that North Vietnam was fighting a total war for national survival and was willing to accept massive casualties (famously declaring: "You can kill ten of my men for every one I kill of yours, yet even at those odds, you will lose and I will win"). The "body count" metric led to fabricated casualty reports, masked political defeat, and destroyed the morale of US troops who captured hills only to abandon them days later.</li></ol>',
