            '<ol><li><strong>[01:30] Recall:</strong> Where did the "Hard Hat Riot" take place on 8 May 1970, and which iconic trade union members attacked student protesters?</li><li><strong>[03:20] Explain:</strong> Why did blue-collar trade union workers feel intense resentment towards college-educated anti-war demonstrators during the Vietnam War?</li><li><strong>[04:50] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate how President Nixon exploited the cultural and class resentments epitomized by the Hard Hat Riot to forge a new conservative Republican electoral coalition.</li></ol>',
          model_answer:
            '<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Lower Manhattan (near Wall Street and Federal Hall), New York City; construction workers belonging to the AFL-CIO building trades unions.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Blue-collar workers viewed college students as privileged, draft-exempt elitists who burned American flags and spat on the military while working-class sons—who could not afford college draft deferments—were conscripted and dying in Vietnamese jungles. Construction workers regarded public protest as an unpatriotic betrayal of their fighting brothers, marching with American flags under the slogan "All the way, USA!"</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Nixon recognized that working-class white union members—traditionally staunch New Deal Democrats—felt deeply alienated by countercultural radicalism, urban riots, and anti-war protests. By welcoming union leaders Peter J. Brennan and construction workers to the White House (accepting an honorary hard hat), Nixon brilliantly peeled white blue-collar "Reagan Democrats" away from the Democratic Party, laying the foundation for his 49-state landslide re-election in 1972.</li></ol>',
